    "report": "Hyphenated phone numbers are now recognised as contact details. The change is committed on `main` as `af1162d`, and nothing is pushed.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}` in both places that hold it:\n  - `src/Contexts/Reviews/Domain/StayReview/ReviewRules.ts`, which refuses `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n  - `src/Contexts/Messaging/Domain/Conversation/ContactDetails.ts`, which holds numbers back as `[hidden]`.\n- ADR 0063 says the phone rule is kept per context, so I changed both rather than sharing a helper.\n- I added tests in both `.spec.ts` files. They cover `06-12-34-56-78`, `+33-6-12-34-56-78`, a mixed separator case, and `2026-12-01` staying allowed. They also check that a double hyphen (`06--12-…`) is not accepted.\n\n**Verification**\n- Unit tests: 155 suites, 1054 tests pass.\n- E2E: 114 suites, 607 tests pass. This ran the whole e2e set, not only the two I named, because the path filter was not applied.\n- `yarn lint`, `yarn typecheck`, `yarn format:check` and `yarn check:conventions` all pass.\n- My first attempt to run a single spec failed because I skipped the project's `--experimental-vm-modules` flag. I then used `yarn test`, which sets it, and confirmed the same spec passes on the untouched tree.\n\n**Decisions and unclear points**\n- **Adjacent dates can now match.** With hyphens allowed, two dates separated by one space, such as `2026-12-01 2026-12-04`, make 16 digits in a chain and are refused or held back. A single date is not affected. The request's rule as written allows this, so I did not add an exception. If that is not wanted, it needs a date-aware rule and a decision from you.\n- **A date followed by a number with a space can match too.** For example, `2026-12-01 2026` gives 9 digits. Same cause, same reasoning.\n- I did not touch ADR 0055 or 0063 or the storm file. They describe phone numbers in general terms and stay accurate.",
